I was recently asked what the cooperative system means to me.&#xA0;&#xA0;
Having grown up on my family&apos;s farm, I believe in a business model that&apos;s owned by the people it serves. That&apos;s what cooperatives are all about. They&apos;re owned by farmers, guided by farmers and focused on creating long-term value for farmers.&#xA0;&#xA0;
A few thoughts in this video as we recognize National Cooperative Month.

Film photography -- an art form that&#x2019;s more than 150 years old -- has been able to evolve and adapt, enabling it to thrive in a modern era. 

<a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/landolakesinc?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>Land O&apos;Lakes, Inc.</a> has lasted more than 100 years because of the durability of the cooperative model, the resiliency of our member-owners and their willingness to evolve and adapt. 

With traditions rooted in generations of family farming, they&#x2019;re able to augment age-old farming methods with cutting-edge technology to keep up with the demands of a modern, global food system.&#xA0; 

&#x201C;I think of it often when I&#x2019;m working in the field. I know that field has been worked by someone in my family for the last 150 years, and I&#x2019;ve got a grandkid riding with me in the tractor. That&#x2019;s what American agriculture is all about. It&#x2019;s more than an occupation. It&#x2019;s a lifestyle and commitment to feeding the planet.&#x201D;

