Securing Uber reaches far beyond the app on your phone.

3 months into the role, <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/philipmmartin?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>Philip Martin</a>, our Chief Information Security Officer, shares 3 things that have stood out &#x1F447;

&#x1F310; The range of problems to solve
&quot;Most people know Uber through rides or delivery. Since joining, I&#x2019;ve gotten a much deeper appreciation for the range of problems behind that experience: identity, payments, infrastructure, AI, and an increasingly connected physical world. If you like working across disciplines, there&#x2019;s an extraordinary amount to dig into here.&quot;

&#x1F916; The chance to rethink security with AI
&quot;What&#x2019;s exciting here is that we get to work on both sides of AI: helping Uber use it responsibly, and using it to make security itself better. That creates an opportunity to rethink how we identify risk, investigate threats, and respond, not just make the same processes a little faster.&quot;

&#x1F49A; The impact of security here is unusually tangible
&quot;Our work connects directly to people&#x2019;s everyday lives. Protecting this platform means protecting the trust of someone getting home, a courier making a delivery, or a driver earning a living. The scale is impressive; the human connection is what makes it meaningful.&quot;

